    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "DTBC-0055 - Download restrictions must be configured."
      info        : "Setting the policy means users cannot bypass download security decisions. There are many types of download warnings within Chrome, which roughly break down into these categories:
- Malicious, as flagged by the Safe Browsing server.
- Uncommon or unwanted, as flagged by the Safe Browsing server.
- A dangerous file type (e.g., all SWF downloads and many EXE downloads).

Setting the policy blocks different subsets of these, depending on its value:

0 = No special restrictions. Default.
1 = Block malicious downloads and dangerous file types.
2 = Block malicious downloads, uncommon or unwanted downloads, and dangerous file types.
3 = Block all downloads.
4 = Block malicious downloads. Recommended."
      solution    : "If the system is on the SIPRNet, this requirement is Not Applicable.

Windows group policy:
1. Open the group policy editor tool with gpedit.msc.
2. Navigate to Policy Path: Computer Configuration\Administrative Templates\Google\Google Chrome\
Policy Name: Allow download restrictions
Policy State: 1, 2, or 4
Policy Value: N/A"
      reference   : "800-171|3.3.1,800-171|3.3.2,800-171r3|03.03.03a.,800-53|AU-12a.,800-53r5|AU-12a.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-000169,CSF|DE.CM-1,CSF|DE.CM-3,CSF|DE.CM-7,CSF|PR.PT-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-01,CSF2.0|DE.CM-03,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-04,DISA_Benchmark|Google_Chrome_Current_Windows,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(b),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.15,ITSG-33|AU-12a.,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|10.1,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-221588r1106670_rule,STIG-ID|DTBC-0055,STIG-Legacy|SV-94635,STIG-Legacy|V-79931,SWIFT-CSCv1|6.4,Vuln-ID|V-221588"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_Google_Chrome_V2R11_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "1" || "2" || "4"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Google\Chrome"
      reg_item    : "DownloadRestrictions"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "DTBC-0057 - Safe Browsing Extended Reporting must be disabled."
      info        : 'Enables Google Chrome\'s Safe Browsing Extended Reporting and prevents users from changing this setting. Extended Reporting sends some system information and page content to Google servers to help detect dangerous apps and sites.
If the setting is set to \'True\', then reports will be created and sent whenever necessary (such as when a security interstitial is shown).
If the setting is set to \'False\', reports will never be sent.
If this policy is set to \'True\' or \'False\', the user will not be able to modify the setting.
If this policy is left unset, the user will be able to change the setting and decide whether to send reports or not.'
      solution    : 'Windows group policy:
1. Open the \'group policy editor\' tool with gpedit.msc
2. Navigate to Policy Path: Computer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Google\\Google Chrome\\Safe Browsing settings\\
Policy Name: Enable Safe Browsing Extended Reporting
Policy State: Disabled
Policy Value: N/A'
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.13,800-171r3|03.13.13,800-53|SC-18(1),800-53r5|SC-18(1),CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001166,CSF|DE.CM-5,DISA_Benchmark|Google_Chrome_Current_Windows,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-18(1),NIAv2|SU3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|8.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-221590r961083_rule,STIG-ID|DTBC-0057,STIG-Legacy|SV-96299,STIG-Legacy|V-81585,Vuln-ID|V-221590"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_Google_Chrome_V2R11_STIG.zip"
      value_type  : POLICY_DWORD
      value_data  : "0"
      reg_key     : "HKLM\Software\Policies\Google\Chrome"
      reg_item    : "SafeBrowsingExtendedReportingEnabled"
      reg_option  : CAN_NOT_BE_NULL
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : REGISTRY_SETTING
      description : "DTBC-0058 - WebUSB must be disabled."
      info        : 'Allows you to set whether websites are allowed to get access to connected USB devices. Access can be completely blocked, or the user can be asked every time a website wants to get access to connected USB devices.
If this policy is left not set, \'3\' will be used, and the user will be able to change it.
2 = Do not allow any site to request access to USB devices via the WebUSB API
3 = Allow sites to ask the user to grant access to a connected USB device'
      solution    : 'Windows group policy:
 1. Open the \'group policy editor\' tool with gpedit.msc
 2. Navigate to Policy Path: Computer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Google\\Google Chrome\\Content Settings
 Policy Name: Control use of the WebUSB API
 Policy State: Enabled
 Policy Value: 2'
